{"task_id": "rs_eval_006", "text": "Implement a stack using a Vec in Rust", "semantic_intent": "coding_implementation", "domain": "coding_rust", "action_target": "coding", "policy_regime": "default", "language_channel": "english", "code_language": "rust", "split": "eval", "expected_response": "A stack is LIFO. Vec already provides push and pop methods. Wrap it in a struct for type safety.", "expected_code": "struct Stack<T> { data: Vec<T> }\nimpl<T> Stack<T> {\n    fn new() -> Self { Stack { data: Vec::new() } }\n    fn push(&mut self, val: T) { self.data.push(val); }\n    fn pop(&mut self) -> Option<T> { self.data.pop() }\n    fn peek(&self) -> Option<&T> { self.data.last() }\n    fn is_empty(&self) -> bool { self.data.is_empty() }\n}"}
{"task_id": "rs_eval_007", "text": "Write a function to flatten a nested vector in Rust", "semantic_intent": "coding_implementation", "domain": "coding_rust", "action_target": "coding", "policy_regime": "default", "language_channel": "english", "code_language": "rust", "split": "eval", "expected_response": "Use into_iter and flatten, or flat_map for transformation. The standard library provides these as iterator adapters.", "expected_code": "fn flatten<T: Clone>(nested: Vec<Vec<T>>) -> Vec<T> {\n    nested.into_iter().flatten().collect()\n}"}
